      "math_academy_id": "q-83921",
      "knowledge_point_id": "566f4fc1-ba4d-5433-9812-a3f5470e3d1e",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Containing a Greater Than Symbol",
      "problem": "Given that the discrete random variable $T$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(t)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(T\\ge 6).$\n\n| $t$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(t)$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ |",
      "worked_solution": "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $T$ is a function $F(t)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(t)=P(T\\le t).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(T>a)=1-P(T\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we want to compute $P(T\\ge 6).$ However, this interval is stated in the form $T\\ge 6,$ not $T>6.$\n\nSo first, we must convert the interval $T\\ge 6$ to the form $T>a.$ Looking at the given possible values of $T,$ we see that $T\\ge 6$ is equivalent to $T>3.$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T\\ge 6) & =P(T>3) \\\\  & =1-P(T\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-F(3) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
